                <pb facs="0352" n="318" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v15p1/pg318"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">795.00/6–1052: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The United States Deputy Representative at the United
                        Nations (Gross) to the
                            Department of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top
                            secret<lb/>priority</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">New
                                York</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1952-06-10T17:10:00-04:00">June 10, 1952—5:10
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>913. For Hickerson (UNA). Suggestion by Kennan in Moscow’s No. 1966, June 9,<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d173-f1"/> that Dept consider “discreet, informal contacts” with Sov
                    re Korean armistice tends to confirm my own feeling discussed with you June 5
                    that such contacts carefully studied and timed might be productive and in any
                    event would serve to establish record of effort which would be potentially
                    useful in future negots with our Allies. Such contacts, made here or in Moscow,
                    should safeguard two tactical principles: (1) Avoid direct or indirect
                    commitment on our part, and (2) leave Sov easy exit so that they would, as
                        Kennan suggests, be in a position to
                    assure China in case of leaks or queries that they have had no discussions with
                    us.</p>
                <p>Possible methods of approach for Dept’s consideration might be: (1) Leading
                        Malik (possibly at his SC dinner this month) into very general conversation
                    on Korea during course of which POW subject
                    could be raised in most casual manner, further conversation on issue being
                    subject to response, if any, from him; or (2) similar approach to Zinchenko at Malik dinner; or (3) discussion in Moscow.</p>
                <p>As to (3), I am of course not in position to make suggestions as to how or when
                    suitable opportunity might arise or be created.</p>
                <p>Advantage of courses (1) or (2) would be: (<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>)
                    Contact would be of routine and “natural” character, and (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">b</hi>) any interesting development could be promptly
                    followed up in similar routine manner.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Gross</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
